      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers, we consider that the last digit can be any of the 5 values (1, 3, 5, 7, or 9) because they are all odd.\nThe first digit can be any value from 1000 to 9999 with the restriction that the number cannot end in 0.\nFrom 1000 to 9999, there are a total of 9000 numbers (1000 to 9999 is 10000 numbers minus the 0, which leaves 9000).\nSince we don't want to count numbers that end in an even digit when multiplied by 5, we need to take out the bad cases.\nFor example, $A = 1000$.\nWhen multiplied here, 50 ends with a value of 0. Thus, we have to take these cases out.\nAfter doing so, $B = 900$. A good case is when we multiply a number by 5 that ends with an even digit.\nThe answer is 9000+900=9900$\\boxed{}$",
